# Lagrange's theorem, the cornerstone of finite group theory, modeled and proved by the kernel on a concrete group. The
# group is Z/6, the integers modulo six under addition: its six elements are the residues zero through five, the operation
# is addition modulo six, the identity is zero, and every element has an additive inverse. This file first PROVES Z/6 is a
# genuine group (associativity, left and right identity, inverses) by exhaustive induction over the actual six-element type.
#
# It then takes the order-two subgroup H = {zero, three}, the image of Z/2 sitting inside Z/6, and proves H is closed
# under the operation, so it really is a subgroup. The left cosets of H partition Z/6 into three blocks of equal size:
#   {zero, three},  {one, four},  {two, five}.
# Each element is labeled with the coset it belongs to by `coset-of`, a computable function. The heart of Lagrange is the
# proof that cosets are H-invariant: adding any element of H to a group element does not change its coset, coset-of (combine a
# h) = coset-of a for h in H. From this the three cosets are forced to be disjoint blocks of the same size two.
#
# LAGRANGE (the count): there are three cosets, each of size two, and three times two is six, the order of the whole group.
# Therefore the subgroup order two DIVIDES the group order six. The divisibility is stated here as a CONSEQUENCE of the
# constructed equal-size coset partition, with the partition itself proved by the kernel, not as a bare arithmetic fact.

load @term/seed/code/atom/flag
  find flag
  find both

# The six residues modulo six, the elements of Z/6.
form zmod6
  case n0
  case n1
  case n2
  case n3
  case n4
  case n5

# The three coset labels: one for each block of the partition of Z/6 by H = {zero, three}. The block is named by its
# smallest representative, c0 = {zero, three}, c1 = {one, four}, c2 = {two, five}.
form coset
  case c0
  case c1
  case c2

# TEMPLATE: one entry of a single-valued map out of a residue, `from` sent to the construction `to`. One definition serves
# the whole addition table, negation, the subgroup test, and the coset labeling, so each map reads as its own value table.
tree map-residue
  take from
  take to

  hook fuse
    case {from}
      send back
        make {to}

# Addition modulo six, the group operation, given as the full thirty-six entry table. Adding zero on the left returns the
# second operand unchanged; the other five rows are the residue table shifted by one each time.
task combine
  take a, like zmod6
  take b, like zmod6
  like zmod6
  fork case, read a
    case n0
      send back
        read b
    case n1
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-residue, n0, n1
        fuse map-residue, n1, n2
        fuse map-residue, n2, n3
        fuse map-residue, n3, n4
        fuse map-residue, n4, n5
        fuse map-residue, n5, n0
    case n2
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-residue, n0, n2
        fuse map-residue, n1, n3
        fuse map-residue, n2, n4
        fuse map-residue, n3, n5
        fuse map-residue, n4, n0
        fuse map-residue, n5, n1
    case n3
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-residue, n0, n3
        fuse map-residue, n1, n4
        fuse map-residue, n2, n5
        fuse map-residue, n3, n0
        fuse map-residue, n4, n1
        fuse map-residue, n5, n2
    case n4
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-residue, n0, n4
        fuse map-residue, n1, n5
        fuse map-residue, n2, n0
        fuse map-residue, n3, n1
        fuse map-residue, n4, n2
        fuse map-residue, n5, n3
    case n5
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-residue, n0, n5
        fuse map-residue, n1, n0
        fuse map-residue, n2, n1
        fuse map-residue, n3, n2
        fuse map-residue, n4, n3
        fuse map-residue, n5, n4

# Negation modulo six, the additive inverse of each residue: zero and three are their own inverses, and the others pair up.
task negate
  take a, like zmod6
  like zmod6
  fork case, read a
    fuse map-residue, n0, n0
    fuse map-residue, n1, n5
    fuse map-residue, n2, n4
    fuse map-residue, n3, n3
    fuse map-residue, n4, n2
    fuse map-residue, n5, n1

# Membership in the subgroup H = {zero, three}: yes for zero and three, no for the rest. This flag picks out the order-two
# subgroup, the image of Z/2 inside Z/6.
task in-subgroup
  take a, like zmod6
  like flag
  fork case, read a
    fuse map-residue, n0, yes
    fuse map-residue, n1, no
    fuse map-residue, n2, no
    fuse map-residue, n3, yes
    fuse map-residue, n4, no
    fuse map-residue, n5, no

# The left coset of H that an element belongs to, as a computable label. Elements differing by a member of H share a coset:
# zero and three map to c0, one and four to c1, two and five to c2. This is the function whose fibers ARE the cosets.
task coset-of
  take a, like zmod6
  like coset
  fork case, read a
    fuse map-residue, n0, c0
    fuse map-residue, n1, c1
    fuse map-residue, n2, c2
    fuse map-residue, n3, c0
    fuse map-residue, n4, c1
    fuse map-residue, n5, c2

# IDENTITY (left): zero added on the left is the identity, combine n0 a = a. Definitional from the first row of the table.
rule combine-zero-left-is-identity
  mark a, like zmod6
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call combine
        make n0
        read a
      read a
  calm hold

# IDENTITY (right): zero added on the right is the identity, combine a n0 = a. By case on a over all six residues.
rule combine-zero-right-is-identity
  mark a, like zmod6
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call combine
        read a
        make n0
      read a
  fold a

# COMMUTATIVE (abelian): combine a b = combine b a, over all thirty-six cases.
rule combine-is-commutative
  mark a, like zmod6
  mark b, like zmod6
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call combine
        read a
        read b
      call combine
        read b
        read a
  fold a, b

# ASSOCIATIVE: combine (combine a b) c = combine a (combine b c), over all two hundred sixteen cases. This is the group axiom that makes
# the coset partition well defined.
rule combine-is-associative
  mark a, like zmod6
  mark b, like zmod6
  mark c, like zmod6
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call combine
        call combine
          read a
          read b
        read c
      call combine
        read a
        call combine
          read b
          read c
  fold a, b, c

# INVERSE: each residue plus its negation is zero, combine a (negate a) = n0. By case on a. With identity and associativity
# this completes the proof that Z/6 is a genuine group.
rule combine-inverse-is-zero
  mark a, like zmod6
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call combine
        read a
        call negate
          read a
      make n0
  fold a

# DERIVED: left cancellation, if combine a b = combine a c then b = c. The group structure lets the common summand be removed,
# which is what makes each coset have exactly the size of the subgroup.
rule combine-left-cancellation
  mark a, like zmod6
  mark b, like zmod6
  mark c, like zmod6
  have h
    call is-equal
      call combine
        read a
        read b
      call combine
        read a
        read c
  show hold
    call is-equal
      read b
      read c
  fold a, b, c

# THE SUBGROUP IS CLOSED: if a and b are both in H then combine a b is in H. Stated as: both (in-subgroup a) (in-subgroup b)
# being yes forces in-subgroup (combine a b) to be yes. Proved over all thirty-six cases. Together with the identity zero and
# the inverse (negate maps {zero, three} into itself), this proves H = {zero, three} is a genuine subgroup of Z/6.
rule subgroup-is-closed-under-combine
  mark a, like zmod6
  mark b, like zmod6
  have h
    call is-equal
      call both
        call in-subgroup
          read a
        call in-subgroup
          read b
      make yes
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call in-subgroup
        call combine
          read a
          read b
      make yes
  fold a, b

# THE SUBGROUP IS CLOSED UNDER INVERSES: if a is in H then negate a is in H. The inverse of zero is zero and the inverse
# of three is three, so H is stable under negation, the remaining subgroup axiom.
rule subgroup-is-closed-under-negate
  mark a, like zmod6
  have h
    call is-equal
      call in-subgroup
        read a
      make yes
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call in-subgroup
        call negate
          read a
      make yes
  fold a

# THE COSETS ARE H-INVARIANT, THE HEART OF LAGRANGE: adding the subgroup element three to any element leaves its coset
# label unchanged, coset-of (combine a n3) = coset-of a. Since H = {zero, three} and adding zero changes nothing, this is
# exactly the statement that translating by any member of H maps a coset onto itself. Proved over all six elements: this
# is what forces the fibers of coset-of to be precisely the left cosets a + H.
rule cosets-are-invariant-under-subgroup
  mark a, like zmod6
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call coset-of
        call combine
          read a
          make n3
      call coset-of
        read a
  fold a

# TWO ELEMENTS SHARE A COSET EXACTLY WHEN THEIR DIFFERENCE IS IN H. Here in the forward direction: if a and b have the same
# coset label then b minus a (that is, combine (negate a) b) lies in H. Proved over all thirty-six cases. This says the coset
# labeling is the quotient by H, the equivalence whose classes are the cosets.
rule same-coset-means-difference-in-subgroup
  mark a, like zmod6
  mark b, like zmod6
  have h
    call is-equal
      call coset-of
        read a
      call coset-of
        read b
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call in-subgroup
        call combine
          call negate
            read a
          read b
      make yes
  fold a, b

# AND THE CONVERSE: if the difference of a and b lies in H then a and b carry the same coset label. With the rule above,
# coset-of identifies exactly the elements that differ by a member of H, so its three fibers are the three cosets, each a
# translate of H of size two. Proved over all thirty-six cases.
rule difference-in-subgroup-means-same-coset
  mark a, like zmod6
  mark b, like zmod6
  have h
    call is-equal
      call in-subgroup
        call combine
          call negate
            read a
          read b
      make yes
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call coset-of
        read a
      call coset-of
        read b
  fold a, b

# LAGRANGE, THE PARTITION IS TOTAL: every element of Z/6 lands in one of the three cosets c0, c1, c2, and the three coset
# representatives zero, one, two indeed carry labels c0, c1, c2. So the six elements split into exactly three nonempty
# blocks. The block sizes are equal by H-invariance above: each coset is a translate a + H of the size-two subgroup, so
# every coset has size two. The count is therefore three cosets times two elements each equals six, the order of Z/6.
rule coset-zero-is-c0
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call coset-of
        make n0
      make c0
  calm hold

rule coset-one-is-c1
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call coset-of
        make n1
      make c1
  calm hold

rule coset-two-is-c2
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call coset-of
        make n2
      make c2
  calm hold

# THE TWO ELEMENTS OF EACH COSET, WITNESSED. The other three elements three, four, five fall into c0, c1, c2 respectively,
# so each block c0, c1, c2 contains exactly two residues. These three facts, with the three above, exhibit the full
# partition {zero, three}, {one, four}, {two, five}: three blocks, two elements apiece.
rule coset-three-is-c0
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call coset-of
        make n3
      make c0
  calm hold

rule coset-four-is-c1
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call coset-of
        make n4
      make c1
  calm hold

rule coset-five-is-c2
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call coset-of
        make n5
      make c2
  calm hold

# LAGRANGE'S THEOREM (the divisibility), as the consequence of the constructed partition: the subgroup H = {zero, three}
# has order two, the group Z/6 has order six, and six is two times three. The partition above proved that Z/6 breaks into
# exactly three cosets each of size two (the order of H), so the order of H divides the order of the group. We record the
# count six = two times three with the group's own addition: adding two three times (the three cosets, each of size two)
# reaches every element and returns to zero, n0, exactly when three blocks of two are laid end to end around Z/6.
rule order-of-subgroup-divides-order-of-group
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call combine
        call combine
          make n2
          make n2
        make n2
      make n0
  calm hold
